passages.text:
In the summer of 1858, a long spell of hot weather turned the River Thames into something close to an open sewer. Since 1800, London's population had {{1|grew}} to well over two million, and most of the city's waste flowed, by way of old drains and overflowing {{2|cesspool's,}} straight into the river. Newspapers soon gave the season a name: "the Great Stink."

Doctors at the time disagreed about what caused the city's repeated cholera {{3|outbreaks. Many}} blamed a poisonous "bad air" rising from filth, while a few argued that the disease spread through contaminated drinking water. Either way, the remedy pointed in the same direction: get the sewage out of the river.

Parliament, {{4|whose building stands beside the Thames,}} could hardly ignore the problem; lawmakers reportedly soaked the curtains in chemicals to block the smell. Within weeks, they passed a law giving the city's Metropolitan Board of Works the power and the money to act.

The board's chief engineer, Joseph Bazalgette, already had a plan. He proposed large intercepting sewers that would run alongside the river and catch waste before it reached the water. {{5|The sewers would carry the waste east. They would carry it to outfalls downstream of the city.}} Because much of the land was too flat for gravity alone to keep the flow moving, the system also needed {{6|four, large, pumping}} stations to lift the sewage. Bazalgette also planned for a city larger than the one he saw. {{7|}}

London's population today is more than three times {{8|as large as}} it was in 1858, and many of Bazalgette's tunnels still carry its waste. One of the original pumping stations, at Crossness, {{9|have}} been restored, and visitors can tour its ornate iron engine house. {{10|}}
